  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Scattered showers and a few storms possible \nfrom the Finger Lakes east through the remainder of the \nafternoon, otherwise increasingly drier conditions return \nthrough Saturday.\n\nThe western periphery of persistent stacked low pressure spinning \nover southeastern New England will continue to impact portions of \nour area through the late afternoon/evening hours while slowly \nweakening and ever so slowly starting to exit east off the New \nEngland coast through tonight. Although areas from the Genesee \nValley westward are expected to remain dry, a veil of low cloud \ncover will hang in across western NY through the remainder of the \nafternoon. Further east, deeper moisture combined with diurnal \nheating will keep the threat of widely scattered showers and \npossibly an isolated storm two in the forecast for the Finger Lakes \nregion and points east into the late afternoon/early evening hours.\n\nAs deeper moisture associated with this stubborn area of low \npressure finally begins to exit east and drier/air subsidence \nattendant to high pressure building in from the west, eastern areas \nwill finally dry out tonight, with dry and seasonably warm weather \nfor Friday. Mainly dry and seasonably warm weather is expected to \npersist into Saturday, although a modest uptick in humidity levels \nin advance of the next system combined with diurnal heating may be \nenough to trigger a couple widely scattered showers/storms along any \nlake breeze circulation boundaries Saturday afternoon. \n\nKey Message 2...Showers and thunderstorms develop for the second \nhalf of the weekend bringing the potential for another round of \nheavy rainfall.\n\nAn upper level trough will dig south across the western Great Lakes \nSaturday night, while at the same time be in the process of phasing \nwith a potent mid-level shortwave/attendant surface low diving \nsoutheast into the Ohio Valley. This will result in a \ndeepening/sharpening mid and upper-level trough that will also slow \nits eastward progression. This will allow for a deep southwesterly \nflow to develop with warm/humid air being advected in across the \nregion all the way from the Gulf. This will transport an anomalously \nhigh PWAT airmass northeast into the region late Saturday night and \nSunday with PWAT values possibly approaching 2 inches. In addition, \na 30-40 knot LLJ will move into the region ahead of surface cold \nfront later Saturday night and Sunday. This will not only give a \nboost to the overall forcing, but will result in some breezy to \npossibly windy conditions as well. Taking these aforementioned \nfactors into account, expect a round or rounds of showers and storms \nfrom later Saturday night through at least the day Sunday, and \npossibly into Sunday night for which WPC continues with a Slight \nRisk (Level 2 of 4) for areas east of the Genesee Valley, with a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) across the remainder of western NY.\n\nBeyond Sunday, there remains some disagreement amongst medium range \nguidance as to the overall pattern evolution going into the first \npart of next week. Majority of medium range guidance, including the \nNBM have the deeper moisture and more persistent shower activity \npushing east of the area by or during the day Monday with drying \nconditions. However, just looking at the overall pattern and some \noutliers in the guidance, there is some potential for a cutoff low \nto develop somewhere over or near our region first part of next week \nas well, which would result in more in the way of at least on and \noff showery weather during this timeframe.",
